  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 PM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nNortheast Kansas remains embedded in northwest flow aloft downstream \nof a mid-level ridge centered over the Intermountain West.  Water \nvapor imagery shows the primary jet displaced well to the north \nwhile sfc high pressure slowly shifts southeast into the Mississippi \nValley. Initial clear skies this evening should begin to observe \nincreasing mid and high clouds as another embedded wave is progged \nto develop clusters of showers and storms in Nebraska after \nmidnight, before entering northeast Kansas by sunrise Friday. Latest \n12Z guidance has shifted measurable QPF further east, encompassing \nmore of northeast Kansas and have adjusted pops accordingly. The \nHRRR has been the most robust with the coverage of showers lingering \nthrough the late morning while other guidance dissipates activity \nbefore reaching KS. Low chance pops seem reasonable at this point \nuntil better consistency is seen with the upper forcing. \n\nDeterministic and ensemble guidance are coming into better agreement \nin terms of temperatures as the upper ridge axis amplifies and \nexpands eastward. Low level warm advection returns to the region as \na lee trough deepens over eastern Colorado, backing sfc winds to the \nsouth. H85 temps steadily increase from 25 to 30C by Saturday \nafternoon, supporting highs in the upper 90s to low 100s through \nMonday afternoon. Boundary layer moisture pooling under the thermal \nridge maintains near 70 degrees, resulting in peak heat indices from \n100 to 105. \n\nBy Sunday evening, an upper-level shortwave trough traversing the \nnorthern plains will force a surface cold front south towards \nsouthern NE. The weak front appears to stall somewhere in the region \nas both GEFS and EC ensembles exhibit varying solutions on degree of \nlift and mid level lapse rates overnight into Monday. This weak, \nquasi-westerly flow pattern aloft continues into most of next week, \nplacing most of northeast Kansas on the warm side of the front. \nHighs in the 90s are therefore trending more likely for the area as \nhigher rain chances are confined to far northern Kansas during the \novernight periods.",
